oken_hex(16)`: state the concrete benefit that outweighs the documented coarse-enclosing-span run-merging footgun, given the plan concedes span-alignment does not hold without the out-of-scope root span — or adopt the always-mint variant.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.8,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'it does NOT by itself align the header with rebar's exported spans: rebar opens no root span, so at the boundary nothing is normally recording and the value is minted'","Plan: 'One consequence to accept: under a coarse enclosing span, several gate runs inherit one id and Langfuse will show them as a single trace'","AC line 2 makes this consequence load-bearing ('under one coarse caller-owned span ... every run inside it reads that span's id, so the values legitimately match') — i.e. the span-read weakens the per-run-distinctness guarantee the ticket titles ('per-gate-run correlation identity')"],"finding":"The approach reads the active OpenTelemetry span's trace id when one is recording, but the plan's own analysis establishes this buys no real correlation benefit (rebar opens no root span, so at the boundary nothing is normally recording, and tracing.py's per-candidate spans are each their own trace root) while it does introduce a documented failure mode: under one coarse caller-owned span every gate run inside it inherits a single id and Langfuse shows them merged. A structurally simpler alternative — always mint `secrets.token_hex(16)` and never read the span — would eliminate that run-merging footgun at no loss to the stated goal (a per-run correlation identity plus a populated trace_id), since the plan concedes span-alignment does not hold without the out-of-scope root span.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fcb8af3f8b0e85324","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section — '32-hex is W3C trace-id format ... Reading a live span's id is correct when an enclosing span exists ... One conse